# description
fpsync is a powerful migration tool used to boost migration of several PB of data and tested by organizations such as UCI, Intel and Amazon.
In addition to being very fast (as transfers start during FS crawling and are parallelized), fpsync is able to resume or replay synchronization "runs"
(see options -r and -R) and presents an overall progress status. It also has a small memory footprint compared to rsync itself when migrating
filesystems with a big number of files.

fpsync is shell script that wraps fpart and rsync, cpio, pax or tar to launch synchronization jobs in parallel, using  GNU Parallel to schedule transfers,
fpsync provides its own -embedded- scheduler. It can execute several synchronization processes locally or launch them on several nodes (workers) through SSH.

# dependencies
fpart, rsync/cpio/pax/tar, a POSIX shell, sudo and ssh.

# install
spack install fpart
# docs
http://www.fpart.org/fpsync/

# fpsync usage
$ fpsync -n 4 -f 1000 -s $((100 * 1024 * 1024)) /data/src/ /data/dst/  #will synchronize /data/src/ to /data/dst/ using 4 local workers, each xfer <=1000 files and 100MB/job.
$ fpsync -n 8 -f 1000 -s $((100 * 1024 * 1024)) -w login@machine1 -w login@machine2 -d /mnt/nfs/fpsync /data/src/ /data/dst/ #  as above but use 8 concurrent sync jobs on two machines
#(machine1 and machine2). Those machines must both be able to access /data/src/ and /data/dst/, as well as /mnt/nfs/fpsync, which is fpsync's shared working directory.

# fpart
UNIX tool that splits a list of directories and file trees into N partitions, trying to produce partitions
with the same size and number of files. It can also produce partitions with a given number of files or of a
limited size. Fpart uses a bin packing algorithm to optimize space utilization amongst partitions.

# fpart usage
$ fpart -n 3 -o var-parts /var
$ ls var-parts*
var-parts.1 var-parts.2 var-parts.3
$ head -n 2 var-parts.1
/var/some/file1
/var/some/file2

# fpart for copying -- explained
# max 10000 files/partition
# exclude .snapshot .zfs
# use /tmp/part.out to write out partition
# use parallel-sem to schedule max 3 jobs at a time
# use rsync to actually copy
$ cd /data/src
$ fpart -L -f 10000 -x '.snapshot' -x '.zfs' -zz -o /tmp/part.out -W '/usr/local/bin/sem -j 3 "/usr/local/bin/rsync -av \
  --files-from=${FPART_PARTFILENAME}  /data/src/ /data/dest/"' .
